  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>If we see the atomic mass versus atomic volume curve, we will notice the following features:</p><ul>\t<li>Alkali metals such as <math> <mrow>  <mtext>Na,</mtext><mtext>&#x2004;</mtext><mtext>K,</mtext><mtext>&#x2004;</mtext><mtext>Rb</mtext></mrow></math> which have similar properties occur as peaks of the curve.</li>\t<li>Halogen elements like <math> <mrow>  <mtext>F,</mtext><mtext>&#x2004;</mtext><mtext>Cl,</mtext><mtext>&#x2004;</mtext><mtext>Br,</mtext></mrow></math> which have similar properties occur at the rising or the ascending part of the curve.</li>\t<li>Noble gases such as <math> <mrow>  <mtext>Ne,</mtext><mtext>&#x2004;</mtext><mtext>Ar,</mtext><mtext>&#x2004;</mtext><mtext>Kr</mtext></mrow></math> which have similar properties occur just before the alkali elements.</li>\t<li>Hydrogen and Helium seem to be exception to the rule.</li></ul><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/DPT_Lothar-Meyer_3.jpg\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 300px;\" /></p>",
